<!--
  ===================================================================
  1. Manager connection and enrollment
  ===================================================================

  The <enrollment> block with an explicit <agent_name> is the important
  part, and it was added only after an incident.

  Changing <address> does NOT change an agent's identity — identity comes
  from enrollment and the client key. When the agent re-enrolled during
  unrelated network churn with no <agent_name> set, Wazuh fell back to the
  Windows hostname and silently created a SECOND agent entry. The original
  went 'disconnected' and looked like a network fault.

  Pin the name at build time. See docs/troubleshooting.md §6.
-->
<client>
  <server>
    <address><WAZUH_SERVER_IP></address>
    <port>1514</port>
    <protocol>tcp</protocol>
  </server>

  <enrollment>
    <enabled>yes</enabled>
    <manager_address><WAZUH_SERVER_IP></manager_address>
    <port>1515</port>
    <agent_name>WIN11-01</agent_name>
  </enrollment>
</client>


<!--
  ===================================================================
  2. Sysmon collection  —  LAB-001
  ===================================================================

  Native Windows logging says a process started. Sysmon Event ID 1 carries
  the full command line, the parent image and the process hashes, which is
  what the custom rules 100100-100103 match on. Without this block those
  rules have nothing to read.

  Sysmon itself runs in its DEFAULT configuration here
  (Sysmon64.exe -accepteula -i) — no curated ruleset. That keeps the
  pipeline simple while the rule logic is being validated; swapping in a
  curated config is Phase 2 work.

  Note: Wazuh maps Sysmon Event ID 1 to built-in rule 61603, which is
  LEVEL 0 and raises no alert by design. Telemetry arriving correctly and
  an empty Threat Hunting view are entirely compatible.
-->
<localfile>
  <location>Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational</location>
  <log_format>eventchannel</log_format>
</localfile>


<!--
  ===================================================================
  3. File Integrity Monitoring  —  LAB-003
  ===================================================================

  Goes INSIDE the existing <syscheck> block. The block below shows it in
  context; copy only the <directories> line into your own <syscheck>.

    check_all      size, permissions, owner, group, mtime, inode,
                   MD5/SHA-1/SHA-256, attributes. This is what makes the
                   before/after hash chain possible.
    realtime       report changes as they happen, not on the next scan.
    report_changes store a content diff, not just "the hash moved".
                   It stores file content on the manager — enable it for
                   specific sensitive paths, never blanket, and never for
                   paths that could hold secrets.

  The monitored directory must EXIST BEFORE the agent restarts.

  Confirm it was loaded by looking for this line in ossec.log:
    INFO: (6003): Monitoring path: 'c:\securitylab', with options
    'size | permissions | owner | group | mtime | inode | hash_md5 |
     hash_sha1 | hash_sha256 | attributes | report_changes | realtime'.
-->
<syscheck>
  <directories check_all="yes" report_changes="yes" realtime="yes">C:\SecurityLab</directories>
</syscheck>


<!--
  -------------------------------------------------------------------
  3b. Who-data  —  ATTEMPTED, NOT OPERATIONAL on this endpoint
  -------------------------------------------------------------------

  Adding whodata="yes" was intended to attribute each file change to a
  user, process and PID:

      <directories check_all="yes" report_changes="yes" realtime="yes"
                   whodata="yes">C:\SecurityLab</directories>

  Every Windows prerequisite was verified independently — File System and
  Handle Manipulation auditing both 'Success' (queried by GUID, because
  English subcategory names are rejected on a localized install), a SACL
  created manually on the directory and confirmed to persist across
  restarts, and auditpol /backup succeeding with exit code 0.

  The agent still failed inside its own audit-policy initialization:

      ERROR: (6955): Auditpol command failed, attempt number: 1
      ERROR: (6915): Audit policies could not be auto-configured due to
                     the Windows version.
      ERROR: (6916): Local audit policies could not be configured.
      ERROR: (6710): Failed to start the Whodata engine.
      Directories/files will be monitored in Realtime mode
      ERROR: (6646): DeleteAce() failed restoring the SACLs. Error '87'

  Wazuh falls back to realtime mode, which is why FIM kept working. The
  fallback is visible in the alert data too: syscheck.mode = realtime.

  Root cause is SUSPECTED, not proven — localization and a very recent
  Windows 11 build are both plausible and the evidence separates neither.
  The controlled experiment (enable whodata on the English endpoint and
  compare) has not been run. See labs/LAB-003-file-integrity-monitoring.md.
-->


<!--
  ===================================================================
  4. Security Configuration Assessment  —  LAB-004
  ===================================================================

  SCA is enabled by default in the agent; the setting that matters for the
  lab is scan_on_start, which makes a restart trigger a fresh assessment.
  That is how the remediation in LAB-004 was independently re-verified:

      net accounts /minpwage:1     (remediate)
      net stop wazuh ; net start wazuh   (force a rescan)
      -> rule 19010, previous_result: failed / result: passed

  The CIS Windows 11 policy ships with the agent at:
      C:\Program Files (x86)\ossec-agent\ruleset\sca\cis_win11_enterprise.yml
-->
<sca>
  <enabled>yes</enabled>
  <scan_on_start>yes</scan_on_start>
  <interval>12h</interval>
</sca>


